#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_function

#% text
# `inverse_function(f)` gives the inverse of the function f.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Named elementary functions map to their inverses (`sin` to `asin`, `exp` to `log`, ...). For a function of one argument the equation f(y) == x is solved and the first solution, its arbitrary constants set to 0, becomes a function of x.

#% item
# `inverse_function(f, k, n)` is the inverse of a function of n arguments in its k-th argument: applied to n arguments, it gives the y with f(.., y, ..) equal to the k-th of them.

#% item
# An inverse without a closed form, applied to real numbers, gives the real solution of the equation when it is the only real one (as solve over the reals finds it, a root object; functions such as `beta(y, 4)` are expanded first when that helps). Otherwise `inverse_function(f)` stays as it is.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_function(sin)
#% output
#= asin

#% cell
inverse_function(t => 2*t + 3)(x)
#% output
#= (1/2)*(-3 + x)

#% cell
inverse_function(t => tan(exp(t)))(x)
#% output
#= log(atan(x))

#% cell
inverse_function(t => (a*t + b)/(c*t + d))(x)
#% output
#= (-b + d*x)/(a - c*x)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The equation sin(y) - y == 2 has no closed-form solution; its one real solution:

#% cell
inverse_function(&(sin(&1) - &1))(2)
#% output
#= root([&(2 + &1 - sin(&1)), -2.554195952837043])

#% text
# The inverse in the first of two arguments: the real x with beta(x, 4) - x + 4 == 3:

#% cell
g = inverse_function((x, y) => beta(x, y) - x + y, 1, 2); n(g(3, 4), 20)
#% output
#= 1.176590107882798171135355

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `solve`, `asin`, `log`
